1
00:00:02,501 --> 00:00:08,501
Hi, I'm Mangesh. Operators lose hours digging through inspections, repair reports and SOPs.

2
00:00:08,504 --> 00:00:14,504
I built a copilot that answers in seconds, with the evidence and the right visual.

3
00:00:14,513 --> 00:00:21,513
The core idea: the language model never does arithmetic. A loader puts every dataset into SQLite.

4
00:00:21,524 --> 00:00:28,524
It extracts the rules from the manuals and SOPs, even containment times hidden in free-text repair reports.

5
00:00:28,529 --> 00:00:35,529
Deterministic, tested tools compute every number, and one composable query covers unseen questions.

6
00:00:35,538 --> 00:00:42,538
Open-weight Gemma 4 plans when the rules aren't sure, writes summaries, and reads inspection photos.

7
00:00:42,545 --> 00:00:48,545
Memory is scoped with provenance, and the copilot is honest when people disagree with the data.

8
00:00:48,554 --> 00:00:55,554
This is the app. On the right, a proactive field briefing: breaches, open fines, long downtime, stale status.

9
00:00:55,630 --> 00:00:59,630
Every alert cites the record it came from.

10
00:00:59,659 --> 00:01:03,659
Let's run one question end to end, as Michael Turner, a production supervisor.

11
00:01:25,272 --> 00:01:32,272
Four breaches, all from the 11 June spill: SR-01 reported after 41 hours, SR-03 and RC-04 overdue.

12
00:01:32,280 --> 00:01:37,280
SR-02: contained after about 7 hours. That fact only exists in the repair report. Let's open it.

13
00:01:38,514 --> 00:01:45,514
The evidence drawer shows the raw record and highlights the exact sentence in the report.

14
00:01:45,605 --> 00:01:52,605
'How I got this' shows the tool that ran and each rule with the document it came from.

15
00:01:52,637 --> 00:01:58,637
Gemma 4 adds a plain-language summary. It's rejected if it invents any number.

16
00:02:00,681 --> 00:02:06,681
A bar chart when you compare wells: WELL-0121 at 49 days, then WELL-0138 and WELL-0107 at 42.

17
00:02:06,689 --> 00:02:10,689
Now feedback. The AI estimated a spill at 4.5 barrels; the crew measured 5.4.

18
00:02:12,636 --> 00:02:18,636
It keeps the AI's estimate, records who corrected it and why, and re-checks the SOP rules.

19
00:02:18,641 --> 00:02:24,641
At 5.4 barrels the spill is reportable, no notice was filed, so it flags a possible SR-01 breach.

20
00:02:25,259 --> 00:02:28,259
Switch to Laura Simmons, the HSE officer. The correction reaches her too.

21
00:02:30,091 --> 00:02:36,091
WELL-0151 is now listed because of Michael's correction, and the missing notice is flagged as overdue.

22
00:02:38,370 --> 00:02:44,370
Private memory: Jessica has no such conversation, so it never borrows Michael's history.

23
00:02:45,602 --> 00:02:52,602
Tyler says WELL-0175 is running. The latest inspection says otherwise, so it's flagged as a data conflict.

24
00:02:52,608 --> 00:02:57,608
Both statements stay on record; his is marked unconfirmed and it asks him to confirm.

25
00:03:25,951 --> 00:03:32,951
Gemma 4 is multimodal: it reads an inspection photo, and its notes stay advisory next to the records.

26
00:03:32,960 --> 00:03:39,960
For the final hour, one command loads the test data, answers every question and fills in my results page.

27
00:03:39,981 --> 00:03:46,981
Deterministic tools, rules from documents, scoped memory with provenance, and honesty. Thanks for watching.

